  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nCurrently, quasi-zonal flow aloft with surface high pressure to\nour east and a lee side sfc trough to our west. This pattern \nresults in south/southeasterly return flow with daytime mixing \nresulting in a few wind gusts up to around 30 mph over parts of \nwestern North Dakota. Afternoon temperatures are in the 60s\nacross the local area, along with a clear sky. Near critical\nfire weather conditions west where the breezy south/southeast\nwinds coincide with RH values in the 20s. \n\nBroad upper level ridging persists over the central CONUS\nthrough the middle of the the week, with a quasi-zonal flow\npattern remaining over the Northern Plains. Dry weather and \ncontinued above normal temperatures in the 60s result (normal \nhighs for third week of Oct are in the low/mid 50s). Afternoon \nhighs peak Thursday and Friday in the 70s, when the upper ridge \namplifies across the Great Plains in response to high amplitude\ntroughing over the western United States. Would not be\nsurprised to see a few lower 80s Thu or Friday for highs\ndepending on cloud cover or other factors, as boundary layer\ntemperatures warm to around 25C (in comparison, boundary layer\ntemps today are in the low/mid teens C). Also on Friday,\nincreasing south/southwesterly flow aloft will see an embedded\nwave lifting out of the western CONUS trough, resulting in\nshowers with some instability and shear present. \n\nAforementioned trough lifts northeast across the region early\nthis coming weekend, trending temperatures cooler and increasing\nprecipitation chances. Western CONUS ridge is projected to\nrebuild behind the upper trough thereafter, though our flow\npattern remains uncertain (zonal or northwesterly).",
